# CODEOWNERS — deliberately empty.
#
# No owners are assigned by default. Uncomment and edit a pattern below when you
# actually want review requested from someone.
#
# WHY EMPTY: on a repo where you are the only human, `* @you` does nothing
# useful. GitHub will not let a PR author approve their own PR, so pairing it
# with a required-review rule makes every PR you open permanently unmergeable.
# Without a required-review rule it just auto-requests review from yourself,
# which is noise.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- format ----
#
# Later matches win. Paths are gitignore-style, relative to the repo root.
#
#   *                       @octocat            every file
#   *.py                    @octocat            by extension
#   /docs/                  @octocat @other     a directory (and everything in it)
#   /src/api/               @team-name          multiple owners allowed
#   /migrations/            @dba                narrow ownership of risky paths
#
# Owners must have write access to the repo, or GitHub silently ignores the
# entry. Teams are written @org/team-name.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------- when to use it -----
#
# WORKING WITH SOMEONE ELSE (e.g. your boss reviews your work):
#   *  @their-github-handle
# Combine with a ruleset requiring 1 approving review and "require review from
# Code Owners". Now their approval is actually required.
#
# SOLO, OR JUST YOU AND CLAUDE:
#   Leave this file empty. Required reviews cannot work with one human.
#   Protect main with a ruleset instead -- see README, "Stopping Claude from
#   pushing to main". A required CI status check does the real work: it blocks
#   direct pushes and blocks merging a red PR, without needing a second person.
